Because local variables and formal parameters inhabit the activation record of their parent function, external subprograms cannot read or modify them directly. When an external routine attempts to alter a variable residing in another function's stack frame without an explicit addressing handle, the compiler rejects the expression as an undeclared identifier outside of its permissible scope. Conversely, global variables remain accessible across separate functions, enabling persistent state updates across function invocations.
